Article 49

Criteria for women and children victims of violence requiring safe houses include:

a. their life is threatened;
b. receiving rejection from family or community;
c. requiring intensive services but their residence is relatively far; and/or
d. neglected if not placed in a safe house.

Article 7

Regional Government in the context of preventing violence against women is responsible for:

a. conducting socialization regarding prevention of violence against women to regional apparatus, community leaders, and community organizations;
b. optimizing the role of customary institutions, culture, religion and community organizations in preventing acts of violence; c. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against women;
d. involving community participation in the recovery and reintegration of women victims of violence;
e. developing a database and integrated information system on acts of violence against women; and
f. developing family resilience programs.

Article 43

(1) Complaint handling services for victims of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 42 letter a are carried out by:

a. integrated service center for women and children empowerment; b. hospitals;
c. community health centers;
d. social protection houses for children/shelter homes;
e. advisory body for guidance and preservation of marriage; f. state prosecutor's office;
g. district court;
h. child social welfare institutions;
i. social welfare coordination institutions;
j. elderly welfare institutions;
k. family welfare consultation institutions; and
l. domestic violence response groups. (2) Complaints about acts of violence against women and children can be made by reporters consisting of victims, family, community and/or community organizations in the field of protection of women and children.
(3) Reporters as referred to in paragraph (1) may file complaints:
a. directly;
b. by telephone; and/or
c. by letter.
(4) In addition to handling complaints submitted by reporters, officers at service institutions as referred to in paragraph (1), are also authorized to handle women and children victims of violence obtained through other information.

Article 41

(1) The implementation of handling women and children victims of violence is carried out through integrated services.

(2) Integrated services for women and children victims of violence use the principles:

a. gender responsive;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationship;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of appropriate language understood by victims;
j. fast and simple;
k. empathy; and
l. fulfillment of the rights of women and children.

1.6.text:
Article 58

(1) The community may participate in the implementation of the protection of women and children.

(2) The community as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of, among others, parents, family, ninik mamak, cadiak pandai, alim ulama, bundo kanduang, traditional leaders, religious leaders, mass media, and the business sector.

(3) Community participation is carried out through the following means:

a. optimizing the functions of community organizations in the Region; 29
b. conducting socialization and education on laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children;
c. providing input in the process of drafting laws and regulations in the Region;
d. conducting socialization on the prevention of child marriage; e. carrying out recovery and reintegration of female victims of violence;
f. providing information and/or reporting the occurrence of violence, discrimination, exploitation, and human rights violations against women and children to the competent authorities;
g. supervising the process of handling cases of violence, discrimination, exploitation, and human rights violations against women and children;
h. providing space for children to gather and organize to express themselves, be creative and convey their opinions;
i. supervising children in accessing various information; j. providing safe products for children;
k. contributing to the fulfillment of children's rights through corporate social responsibility; and/or
l. disseminating information and educational materials beneficial to women and children.

Article 3

Every woman has the right:

a. to obtain protection from discriminatory actions; b. to obtain protection from economic and sexual exploitation; c. to obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, and neglect;
d. to obtain work leave while receiving salary or wages in accordance with laws and regulations;
e. to obtain decent work according to her ability; 6
f. to obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and paths of education;
g. ownership and management of joint property in marriage in accordance with laws and regulations;
h. to elect and be elected in general elections in accordance with laws and regulations;
i. to receive special protection in carrying out her work or profession against matters that may threaten her safety and/or health concerning women's reproductive functions; and
j. to obtain health services in accordance with standards.

Article 2

The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:

a. rights of women and children;
b. protection of women;
c. fulfillment of child rights
d. special protection of children;
e. handling of women and children victims of violence;
f. establishment of UPTD PPA;
g. guidance and supervision;
h. community participation; and
i. financing.

Article 6

Regional Government in the effort to protect women is responsible for:

a. providing protection from discriminatory actions in accordance with laws and regulations;
b. providing protection from economic exploitation and social exploitation;
c. providing protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence and neglect;
d. providing equal opportunities with men to obtain employment in accordance with their capabilities;
e. providing work leave while still receiving salary or wages in accordance with laws and regulations;
f. providing adequate health and delivery facilities for pregnant and breastfeeding mothers;
g. providing comprehensive health services free of charge; 8
h. guaranteeing the right of women to vote and be elected in general elections in accordance with laws and regulations;
i. providing special protection in the performance of work or profession against matters that may threaten their safety and or health concerning women's reproductive function;
j. providing special protection to women victims of acts of violence and human trafficking;
k. taking preventive actions in order to prevent violations against women; and
l. making regulations to govern employers who employ women to comply with labor laws and regulations.

